  "text": "umably, the expression $n + 2$ divides the product $3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9)$ for any integer $n$.\n\nTo find the sum of all positive integers $n$ that satisfy this condition, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. Express the division condition mathematically.\n2. Iterate over possible values of $n$ to find those that satisfy the condition.\n3. Sum up all the valid values of $n$.\n\nLet's write the Python code to solve this:\n\n```python\nimport sympy as sp\n\n# Define the variable\nn = sp.symbols('n')\n\n# Define the product\nproduct = 3 * (n + 3) * (n**2 + 9)\n\n# Find the values of n such that n + 2 divides the product\nvalid_n = []\n\n# Iterate over possible values of n\nfor i in range(1, 1000):  # Arbitrarily choosing a large range to find all possible values\n    if product % (i + 2) == 0:\n        valid_n.append(i)\n\n# Sum up all the valid values of n\nresult = sum(valid_n)\n\n# Print the result\nprint(result)\n```\n```output\n268\n```\nThe sum of all positive integers $n$ such that $n + 2$ divides the product $3(n + 3)(n^2 + 9)$ is \\(\\boxed{268}\\).",
